Section~\ref{sec:simulations} compared estimation accuracy at a single sample size. This
section turns to cost per iteration. The comparison with ADMM is not of this kind: ADMM
returns a point estimate and no posterior draws, so a per-iteration comparison is not
meaningful, and the relevant difference is what each method delivers rather than what each
costs. Against \citet{onizuka2024fast} the comparison is direct, since both methods draw
the same object from the same working likelihood.

\paragraph{What each iteration does.}
One sweep of the individual sampler consists of a P-MALA update for $\theta$ followed by the
two conjugate scalar draws for $\lambda$ and $\sigma$. The $\theta$ update evaluates the
proximal operator of the difference penalty three times per sweep --- once for the proposal
and once for each of the forward and reverse drift terms in the acceptance ratio. That
proximal map is a quadratic objective with an $\ell_1$ difference penalty, and is therefore
not the check-loss problem of \citet{brantley2020baseline}, although it is solved by an ADMM
of the same banded form: each inner iteration performs one solve against a factorization of
a banded kernel that is computed once per call and reused across inner iterations. The cost
of a sweep is consequently dominated by the total number of inner iterations rather than by
any dense factorization. Two implementation details qualify this description. Each proximal
call constructs its own operators and factorizations before iterating, so a sweep performs
several such setups rather than none; and the difference operator is stored densely in the
implementation timed here, which contributes a term outside the proximal calls that a
stencil or successive-differencing representation would remove.

One Gibbs sweep of \citet{onizuka2024fast} instead draws $\theta$ directly from its augmented
Gaussian full conditional. That conditional has a banded precision matrix --- half-bandwidth
$k+1$, with the boundary rows of the prior leaving the bandwidth unchanged --- so the draw
can in principle be obtained from one banded Cholesky factorization and two triangular
solves. The implementation supplied with that paper does not take this route: it forms the
explicit inverse of the precision matrix, symmetrizes the resulting dense covariance, and
passes it to a multivariate normal generator that decomposes it afresh at every sweep. This
is not governed by that implementation's \texttt{sparse} option, which changes only how the
difference operator is stored and leaves the $\theta$ draw untouched; the two settings are
reported separately below only to document that the option does not address this cost.

\paragraph{Measured cost at $n = 500$.}
Table~\ref{tab:cost_n500} reports the time per complete iteration on one Gaussian $S$ data
set at $n = 500$, $k = 2$, $\tau = 0.5$. The protocol is three repetitions of thirty measured
iterations, each preceded by five untimed iterations for warm-up only; the reported figure
is the median of the three repetition means, not a median over individual iterations, and
the spread across repetitions is given alongside it. Setup, data generation and output are
excluded; for the individual sampler the figure includes all three proximal calls and their
internal iterations. The warm-up is a timing device and not a convergence claim: the
measured window sits early in the chain.

\begin{table}[!htbp]
\centering
\small
\caption{Time per complete iteration at $n = 500$, $k = 2$, $\tau = 0.5$, $S$ signal with
Gaussian noise; median of three repetition means, with the range across repetitions. The
\citet{onizuka2024fast} rows use the implementation supplied with that paper at its default
\texttt{sparse = FALSE}. Measured on a single machine with R's reference BLAS.}
\label{tab:cost_n500}
\begin{tabular}{lrr}
\toprule
Method & Median ms/iteration & Range across repetitions \\
\midrule
Individual sampler & 30.33 & 30.00--85.33 \\
Onizuka (Laplace) & 435.67 & 418.67--1126.33 \\
Onizuka (Horseshoe) & 649.00 & 424.00--1001.00 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

Across the $90$ measured iterations the individual sampler made $270$ proximal calls
totalling $87{,}201$ inner ADMM iterations, or about $969$ inner iterations per sweep, and
the proximal calls accounted for roughly $97\%$ of timed work. No call reached the iteration
limit; the largest observed count was $581$. The per-iteration ordering at this sample size
is therefore obtained with the proximal updates solved to the solver's own stopping rule
rather than truncated at a fixed budget.

