Table~\ref{tab:cost_scaling} is to report the time per iteration across sample sizes, under
both settings of the competitor's \texttt{sparse} option. Two questions are open until it is
populated. The first is whether the ordering at $n = 500$ persists and how the ratio behaves
as $n$ grows; nothing in this section establishes a trend, since every figure in
Table~\ref{tab:cost_n500} is at one sample size. The second is how much of the difference is
attributable to the supplied implementation rather than to the sampling scheme it
implements. The \texttt{sparse} option cannot answer that, because it does not alter the
$\theta$ draw; answering it requires timing a banded implementation of the same Gaussian
conditional, which is not what the option provides and is not attempted here.

