\paragraph{What these timings do and do not establish.}
Three limitations bound what can be read from this section. First, time per iteration is not
time per effective draw: the individual sampler's $\theta$ update is a
Metropolis-within-Gibbs step, whereas \citet{onizuka2024fast} draw $\theta$ from an exact full
conditional, so equal iteration counts need not carry equal information, and a comparison in
effective sample size requires mixing diagnostics that are not reported here. Second, the
figures measure the computational work of the implementations as supplied, including the
draft form of the P-MALA proposal; they are not a statement about the cost of a kernel whose
invariant distribution has been verified. Third, the two hierarchies differ in boundary
treatment and in their scale and shrinkage priors, so the comparison is between two complete
procedures and not between two implementations of one algorithm.
